dvisvgm: the faces of a TrueType collection are different fonts

NativeFont::uniqueName keys a native font by path and style (embolden,
extend, slant), not by its face index. luaotfload's DVI font names carry the
index ("[Optima.ttc]:index=2", parsed in DVIReader::defineFont) and XeTeX's
XDV font definitions do too, but FontManager::registerFont finds the first
face of the file already registered under the same key and clones it, so
every face draws with the first one's index. With --no-fonts the later faces'
glyphs are <use> references to the first face's paths; with
--font-format=woff2 they all share one @font-face. Unchanged in upstream
dvisvgm 3.6.1. A font that is not a collection always has index 0, so its
name (nf0, nf1, ...) and the SVG it produces are what they were, except that
the order of glyph definitions, which follows heap addresses, can move.

Applied by scripts/build-dvisvgm-wasm.sh to copies under build/dvisvgm/patched;
the vendored tree is never modified. Reproduce the defect by setting faces 0-3
of macOS's /System/Library/Fonts/Optima.ttc as upright, bold, italic and bold
italic with fontspec (FontIndex) and running dvilualatex and dvisvgm.

